# second-brain-workflow machine configuration
#
# Copy to ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/second-brain-workflow/config and edit.
# Format is KEY=value, one per line. A comment must start its own line — a
# trailing `# ...` is not stripped, it becomes part of the value. No shell
# expansion beyond a leading ~, so write ~/vaults/... not $HOME/vaults/...
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#   1. CLI flag        (--vault, --targets)
#   2. environment     (SBW_VAULT, RENDER_TARGETS, ...)
#   3. this file
#   4. built-in defaults

# Obsidian vault this machine reads and writes.
# Default: ~/vaults/second-brain
SBW_VAULT=~/vaults/second-brain

# Which agent formats to render. Comma-separated: cursor, claude-code, agents.
# Personal machine running Cursor:            cursor,agents
# Work machine running Claude Code in VS Code: claude-code,agents
# Default: cursor,claude-code,agents
RENDER_TARGETS=cursor,claude-code,agents

# Where skills get installed. Colon-separated, like PATH.
# Default: ~/.cursor/skills:~/.claude/skills
#
# Narrowing this (a machine running only Claude Code, say) does not remove what
# is already installed elsewhere — it just stops every tool here from looking
# there. Run ./scripts/uninstall.sh before narrowing, or `make doctor`
# afterwards, which reports links of ours left outside this list.
SKILLS_DIRS=~/.cursor/skills:~/.claude/skills

# Which upstream skills from vendor/obsidian-skills to install.
# Space-separated. Available: obsidian-bases obsidian-markdown obsidian-cli
#                             json-canvas defuddle
# Default: obsidian-bases obsidian-markdown
VENDOR_SKILLS=obsidian-bases obsidian-markdown

# Where rules/*.md and AGENTS.md live, if not co-located with this engine
# checkout. Leave unset/empty for a self-contained checkout (rules/ as a
# sibling of scripts/) — the common case for a fresh adopter. Set this when
# rules live in a separate repo, e.g. a private layer on top of a public
# engine checkout.
# Default: <this engine checkout>/rules
SBW_RULES_DIR=

# The vault.json id this machine expects to find when guard-vault-commit.sh
# runs. This is what makes the guard's identity check non-circular: the
# expectation lives here, on the machine, not in vault.json itself — a
# repointed or freshly cloned vault brings its own vault.json along with it,
# so trusting that file to also say what's "correct" would prove nothing.
# No default — guard-vault-commit.sh fails closed (refuses to commit) if this
# is unset and neither --expect-id nor this key resolves to a value. Set it
# once per vault this machine writes to, e.g. "personal" or "work".
SBW_EXPECTED_VAULT_ID=
